"Surah al-Ikhlas is one of the shortest and most concise surahs introducing Allah. Elmalılı states that the surah explains tawhid (the oneness of Allah) in a simple yet very profound manner.\n\nThe command 'Say' demonstrates that this knowledge is not our Prophet's own words, but the truth revealed by Allah. The believer also comes to know Allah through this divine declaration and proclaims it as such.\n\nThe expression 'Allah is One' proclaims that Allah has no peer, partner, likeness, or second deity. This oneness is not merely in terms of number, but in the sense of being unique in His Essence and divinity.\n\nThe name 'al-Samad' conveys that everyone is in need of Allah, while Allah is in need of nothing. All needs are directed to Him; He is flawless, complete, and not dependent on any being.\n\nThe expression 'He neither begets nor is born' refutes notions of lineage, ancestry, and physical comparisons regarding Allah. Allah does not resemble created beings; He is not limited by a beginning or an end.\n\nThe sentence 'Nor is anything equal to Him' completes the culmination of tawhid. According to Elmalılı, the surah exalts Allah above both having partners and resembling created beings.\n\nIn short, Surah al-Ikhlas teaches knowing Allah as One, unique, absolutely free of need, and far removed from all imperfections."